WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.690
Repeatedly listening to, sort
of, the same material is creating

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.880
connections, which is beneficial.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.130
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and today,
I want to talk about why repetition

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.460
is so important in language learning.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:17.480
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.070
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.650
And if you're listening to Apple Podcast
or Spotify, please leave a review.

00:00:27.315 --> 00:00:31.485
So, you know that I'm a
proponent proponent of compelling

00:00:31.485 --> 00:00:33.495
input, comprehensible input.

00:00:34.215 --> 00:00:38.265
Uh, I'm very much a supporter
of, uh, Stephen Krashen's theory

00:00:38.265 --> 00:00:41.955
of language acquisition, but
there are some issues with that.

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:45.945
And some, some of my experience,
which suggests that it's a

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:49.665
little bit sort of more complex.

00:00:50.714 --> 00:00:53.835
First of all, obviously when you
start in a language, whatever you're

00:00:53.835 --> 00:00:55.275
listening to is not comprehensible.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:57.915
So you can't begin with
comprehensible input.

00:00:58.335 --> 00:01:01.875
You'll begin with input, which is not
comprehensible, but which gradually

00:01:01.875 --> 00:01:03.825
becomes more and more comprehensible.

00:01:04.394 --> 00:01:09.225
Uh, however, uh, if you have access
to the text, so you can look up

00:01:09.225 --> 00:01:11.835
words, then you have a chance.

00:01:11.835 --> 00:01:14.925
And if you can use LingQ for example,
and you can review the words, then

00:01:14.925 --> 00:01:18.494
you have a chance, or even if you're
using Teach Yourself or some other

00:01:18.494 --> 00:01:24.015
starter book you can gradually get to
where material that is initially not

00:01:24.015 --> 00:01:26.325
comprehensible becomes comprehensible.

00:01:26.985 --> 00:01:30.345
In order for the material to be
comprehensible, or you have for you

00:01:30.345 --> 00:01:36.375
to have a chance to make that material
comprehensible, uh, you have to have

00:01:36.375 --> 00:01:38.445
stuff that's not that interesting.

00:01:38.925 --> 00:01:42.885
Uh, it's not like tremendously
compelling on its own.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.070
Uh, it's material that's somewhat
simplified because it just,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.840
you need to start in somewhere.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.710
Now with our mini stories at LingQ
we start into everyday situations.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.890
We start, we don't start
in with hello, how are you?

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.690
Uh, my name is...

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.060
uh, but nevertheless it is simplified
compared to, uh, reading, you know, the

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.680
equivalent of Shakespeare or something.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.810
So compelling?

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:07.410
Yes.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.680
Eventually, in the long run.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:13.410
Because in the long run, we have
to accumulate so many words.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:16.109
We have to engage through
reading and listening.

00:02:16.109 --> 00:02:19.679
We have to engage with
genuinely compelling input,

00:02:19.679 --> 00:02:20.970
but we have to get there.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.010
And even when we are engaged with
genuinely compelling input, there are

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:27.750
things that we aren't going to notice.

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:29.869
And that's where repetition comes in.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:38.114
And I remember it's got to be up 20
odd years ago, uh, I met a recent

00:02:38.144 --> 00:02:43.725
immigrant to Canada from China who
had been in Canada for like a year

00:02:44.295 --> 00:02:50.775
and he spoke English almost without
an accent, and not many Chinese people

00:02:51.025 --> 00:02:56.204
manage to do that for whatever reason,
which I won't get into now, but he did.

00:02:56.715 --> 00:03:01.845
And what he said was that he had
this limited material that he

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:05.644
listened to a thousand times,
the same material over and over.

00:03:06.614 --> 00:03:11.250
Um, I know that in my own case
for Chinese, I listened to the

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.350
same material many, many times.

00:03:13.369 --> 00:03:13.739
Another...

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:20.369
in fact, I have over the years developed
this habit of repetitively listening

00:03:20.579 --> 00:03:22.469
to a limited amount of content.

00:03:23.119 --> 00:03:27.045
I do this with mini stories,
Persian, Arabic, Greek,

00:03:27.045 --> 00:03:28.755
whatever I've been learning.

00:03:28.995 --> 00:03:32.625
If I look up my statistics on
LingQ, I'll see that I've listened

00:03:32.625 --> 00:03:35.235
to these stories 30, 40 times.

00:03:35.325 --> 00:03:38.445
I don't listen to them
all at the same time.

00:03:38.445 --> 00:03:40.695
I don't listen to the same
story, 30 times in a row.

00:03:41.385 --> 00:03:46.305
Because the thing about repetition is
we start to lose, you know, the brain...

00:03:46.395 --> 00:03:51.495
I always say this, the brain likes some
novelty, so that's, you've got to move on.

00:03:51.615 --> 00:03:56.475
So typically what I do is I listen
to lesson one or story 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,

00:03:56.475 --> 00:04:02.435
back to one or I, I sort of shuffle
them in my, you know, uh, playlist,

00:04:02.765 --> 00:04:06.275
but in the end, I'll have listened
to them many, many, many times.

00:04:06.815 --> 00:04:12.615
So what I find is that listening
repetitively to a limited amount of

00:04:12.615 --> 00:04:14.565
material has a number of benefits.

00:04:14.685 --> 00:04:21.345
Now I am no neuroscientist, but I
know that, you know, neurons that fire

00:04:21.345 --> 00:04:24.695
together, they form a, a connection.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.180
Can't remember the, there was some kind
of a description of that, that you need

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:34.400
to get these synapses, these neurons to
be firing together repeatedly in order

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.810
to create these new neural connections,
uh, which you need for language.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:44.570
So without knowing the sort of
neuroscience behind it all, I'm quite

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:49.340
convinced that repeatedly listening to
sort of the same material is creating

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.530
connections, which is beneficial.

00:04:51.755 --> 00:04:53.045
Beneficial, not...

00:04:53.245 --> 00:04:59.585
not only for your pronunciation, because
as we know from immigrants who live in,

00:04:59.585 --> 00:05:03.515
say an English speaking country or French
Quebec, French speaking environment

00:05:03.965 --> 00:05:08.855
say, and live 20 years and operate in
English all the time and yet their, their

00:05:08.855 --> 00:05:12.635
pronunciation never improves, but they
are exposed to a lot of the language.

00:05:12.935 --> 00:05:18.005
However, I believe if those same
people had the patience that this

00:05:18.005 --> 00:05:22.335
immigrant had 20 years ago to listen
to a limited amount of content over

00:05:22.335 --> 00:05:26.415
and over and over again, that that
might improve their pronunciation.

00:05:26.805 --> 00:05:30.705
Certainly I find that my
ability to pronounce something

00:05:30.735 --> 00:05:33.045
in a language is much better.

00:05:33.345 --> 00:05:37.815
Uh, three months after, you know, I have
been listening to this repeated content,

00:05:37.815 --> 00:05:39.465
these mini stories over and over again.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.099
It's doing something that I'm not
really conscious of, but is helping

00:05:44.099 --> 00:05:45.930
me to pronounce the language.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:50.900
I recently did a video in
Persian and I had compliments,

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:52.400
at least on my pronunciation.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.090
I have trouble.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:58.430
And I was obviously searching for words,
but my pronunciation isn't bad, partly

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.190
because I've learned many languages
so probably my brain is a little more

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:06.020
flexible, but I also think it's because
I have been listening to these same

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.640
mini stories over and over and over.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:13.970
The other advantage of, there are a number
of advantages, of sort of repeatedly

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:15.770
listening to a limited range of content.

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:16.820
It's easy to do.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.010
You can focus in, focus out.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.290
It's something that I
can do while exercising.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:25.880
Uh, I don't need to be concentrating
and yet still it's working on my brain

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.170
and it's not only the pronunciation
that is kind of being grooved in

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.910
there, it's also a certain structure.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:37.710
Uh, so that all of a sudden these
phrases or structures come out because

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:41.489
I have listened to them over and over
again, while exercising, while doing

00:06:41.489 --> 00:06:44.520
the dishes, even while being in the,
you know, driving the car and so forth.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:51.570
So, uh, repetitive listening, uh, is,
is not compelling input necessarily

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.450
by the time you're on, you're
listening to it for the 30th time.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:07:00.344
It's not compelling, but even the
30th time there are things there

00:07:00.375 --> 00:07:03.734
that I notice, there are phrases
that I notice, there are changes in

00:07:03.734 --> 00:07:05.705
the form of the verb that I notice.

00:07:05.895 --> 00:07:10.724
It might be an issue of intonation
and the language that I notice so

00:07:10.724 --> 00:07:14.445
that there are, you know, aspects of
the language that are subconsciously

00:07:14.474 --> 00:07:20.375
being grooved in my brain, because
these synapses are firing all the time.

00:07:20.375 --> 00:07:23.395
Then pardon me, I'm not a
neuroscience, I'm assuming.

00:07:24.044 --> 00:07:26.085
Uh, and also.

00:07:26.895 --> 00:07:30.405
Uh, there are things that we
notice, so that both subconsciously

00:07:30.735 --> 00:07:36.855
and consciously with a sort of a
limited sampling, we are gaining

00:07:37.034 --> 00:07:39.195
greater confidence in the language.

00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:42.645
And I think repetitive listening
to things like the mini stories

00:07:42.645 --> 00:07:44.534
is great preparation for speaking.

00:07:45.135 --> 00:07:51.645
Uh, whereas, you know, obviously
exploring new stuff more interesting, more

00:07:51.645 --> 00:07:56.475
compelling stuff, especially if we follow
it up with reading is going to expand

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:59.505
our vocabulary, our range of vocabulary.

00:07:59.535 --> 00:08:03.855
But in terms of getting a better handle
on the basic structures of the language

00:08:04.185 --> 00:08:08.295
and the pronunciation of the language,
these call them basics, which I always

00:08:08.295 --> 00:08:13.845
feel can't be learned upfront, but which
can be learned later on that these basics

00:08:14.175 --> 00:08:21.180
are often best acquired through having
a program of repeated listening to a

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:26.880
limited range of material and blending
that in with the sort of more, you know,

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.000
uh, extensive reading and listening.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:34.590
Uh, listening to more compelling material
reading or compelling material, which

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:39.240
is also of course very important,
but even at a fairly advanced level

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:43.350
in the language, I find that this
repeated listening to a limited

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:45.480
range of relatively simple material.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:50.760
And particularly if it's a voice
that we like, uh, even if the content

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:56.430
is not compelling, can be very,
very helpful in strengthening our

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.560
confidence in using the language.

00:08:58.949 --> 00:09:04.890
So there you have a why repetition
is important in language learning.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:05.910
Bye for now.

